From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 10:11:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] clutter/color: Leave PQ highlights unmapped without a
 declared peak
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The effective peak took the encoding's nominal maximum as a bound for
every encoding but extended-range linear. For PQ that maximum is 10000
cd/m², the capacity of the absolute encoding, not the peak of the
content, so plain PQ at the protocol defaults, the common case, got a
BT.2390 knee at about 149 cd/m² on a 600 cd/m² panel: content the panel
can show was compressed, reference white was no longer anchored, and
direct scanout was refused for every plain PQ surface.

Introduce one explicit notion, whether an encoding's nominal maximum
describes the content's peak: it does for SDR transfer functions and for
HLG, whose nominal maximum is a display-referred peak; it does not for
PQ and for extended-range linear encodings. Without MaxCLL or a
mastering maximum the peak of the latter is unknown, and no highlight
mapping applies, as was already the case for ext_linear.

Keep it distinct from the signal-range notion the gamut rule uses,
renamed clutter_color_state_params_is_signal_range_bounded(), each in
one function. Document that the signal range is keyed on the transfer
function's name, and the known gap: gamma-type floating point content
declaring wider mastering primaries is not compressed while its
encoding primaries lie within the target.

Both predicates are exported for the tests. The tests that codified the
old behaviour now expect plain PQ to be unmapped and kept for scanout,
the BT.2390 reference tests declare a MaxCLL, and a new test covers
plain PQ, MaxCLL above and below the target, and HLG at its default
above a 616 cd/m² target.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DLZiLBYev72J1s4D37L7Bi
